WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.820 --> 00:00:04.779
Given A Nightmare on Elm Street's immense
popularity and its status in the modern horror

00:00:04.779 --> 00:00:09.509
film canon, you would think there would be
very few facts or secrets about the 1984 horror

00:00:09.509 --> 00:00:10.690
classic left to discover.

00:00:10.690 --> 00:00:14.360
But thanks to an obscure deleted scene, you
would be oh-so wrong.

00:00:14.360 --> 00:00:18.880
A scrapped scene from the film has recently
resurfaced online, and it completely changes

00:00:18.880 --> 00:00:22.960
everything we thought we knew about the burnt-up
serial killer Freddy Krueger and his most

00:00:22.960 --> 00:00:26.119
well-known would-be victim, 15-year-old Nancy
Thompson.

00:00:26.119 --> 00:00:31.140
The sequence, first included in the 1996 Laserdisc
release of A Nightmare on Elm Street and only

00:00:31.140 --> 00:00:35.960
included sporadically in later home video
releases, is an extended version of the conversation

00:00:35.960 --> 00:00:39.230
Nancy and her mother Marge have in the original
cut of the film.

00:00:39.230 --> 00:00:43.160
Marge takes Nancy down to their basement and
explains to her that Krueger was "a filthy

00:00:43.160 --> 00:00:46.520
child murderer" who was freed on a legal technicality.

00:00:46.520 --> 00:00:50.510
Marge says that she and the other parents
of Springwood, Ohio tracked Krueger down and

00:00:50.510 --> 00:00:54.090
discovered him hiding in a boiler room where
he used to take his young victims.

00:00:54.090 --> 00:00:57.920
The parents then doused the place in gasoline
and lit it up in an attempt to get rid of

00:00:57.920 --> 00:01:01.710
Krueger for good, a strategy echoed by Nancy
at the end of the film..

00:01:01.710 --> 00:01:05.710
In the theatrical version of A Nightmare on
Elm Street, Nancy's mother assures her that

00:01:05.710 --> 00:01:08.640
Krueger can't get to her, and that he's dead
because she's killed him.

00:01:08.640 --> 00:01:12.351
Marge then pulls out one of Krueger's razor
gloves to prove that he's gone and that Nancy

00:01:12.351 --> 00:01:13.351
can rest easy.

00:01:13.351 --> 00:01:16.930
But in this alternate scene, there's additional
exposition between the moment Marge admits

00:01:16.930 --> 00:01:20.930
to burning Krueger alive and when she unwraps
the killer's famous weapon.

00:01:20.930 --> 00:01:24.950
Nancy's mother discloses the real motivation
behind murdering Krueger: she and her fellow

00:01:24.950 --> 00:01:28.890
neighborhood parents wanted revenge after
Krueger had already killed some of their own

00:01:28.890 --> 00:01:29.890
children.

00:01:29.890 --> 00:01:33.399
That's right: Nancy and her friends all had
siblings Krueger had already murdered.

00:01:33.400 --> 00:01:35.320
"You too Nancy.

00:01:37.120 --> 00:01:39.740
You weren't always an only child"

00:01:39.740 --> 00:01:44.090
With those few sentences from her mom, Nancy
realizes that Krueger isn't just some random,

00:01:44.090 --> 00:01:48.659
deranged man with supernatural abilities and
a desire to kill children and teenagers.

00:01:48.659 --> 00:01:52.229
He isn't even just a killer who seeks vengeance
after getting burned alive all those years

00:01:52.229 --> 00:01:53.229
ago.

00:01:53.229 --> 00:01:57.130
No, he's a murderer who took away a piece
of Nancy's family, who left her parents bereft

00:01:57.130 --> 00:02:01.799
and her without a sibling and who also did
the same to her closest friends and their

00:02:01.799 --> 00:02:02.799
families.

00:02:02.799 --> 00:02:04.680
"They all had a brother or sister once."

00:02:04.680 --> 00:02:09.470
As such, this reveal seems to alter the entire
dynamic between Nancy and Krueger and instills

00:02:09.470 --> 00:02:11.950
in the teen a new reason to go after the monster.

00:02:11.950 --> 00:02:15.500
Though the deleted sequence has been readily
available to watch online for years now, there's

00:02:15.500 --> 00:02:19.550
a chance plenty A Nightmare on Elm Street
fans haven't yet seen it which is kind of

00:02:19.550 --> 00:02:22.820
huge considering how drastically it could
change their thinking of both the original

00:02:22.820 --> 00:02:25.930
1984 film and the film series as a whole.

00:02:25.930 --> 00:02:30.320
If Craven had kept this version of the storytelling
session in the theatrical version of A Nightmare

00:02:30.320 --> 00:02:34.390
on Elm Street, it potentially could have had
a domino effect on the events of every franchise

00:02:34.390 --> 00:02:35.920
installment thereafter.

00:02:35.920 --> 00:02:39.400
Each new series entry picks up and expands
on what came before it.

00:02:39.400 --> 00:02:43.420
So if the official Nightmare lore included
the fact that Nancy had a sibling who was

00:02:43.420 --> 00:02:48.190
killed, that could have been used to explain
her reemergence in 1987's A Nightmare on Elm

00:02:48.190 --> 00:02:49.650
Street 3: Dream Warriors.

00:02:49.650 --> 00:02:53.060
It definitely would have provided her with
even more motivation to put Krueger down in

00:02:53.060 --> 00:02:57.220
the classic original Nightmare, as if slipping
her supernatural phone tongue wasn't enough.

00:02:57.800 --> 00:02:58.800
"So it's okay now.

00:03:02.240 --> 00:03:03.240
You can sleep."

